// §3.6.3.2 Net Discipline Initial (Nodeset) Values (Accellera Verilog-AMS,
// docs/ch3-datatypes.html — NOT IEEE 1800.2, which is UVM):
//
//   "The initializer shall be a constant_expression and will be used as a
//    nodeset value for the potential of the net by the analog solver."
//
// tests/fixtures/ch03_data_types/21_net_nodeset.va already pins the negative
// half of that sentence — a nodeset is a HINT, so the net is not clamped to it.
// Nothing anywhere pins the POSITIVE half: that the solver actually starts its
// DC iteration from the declared number. On a circuit with one solution that
// claim is unobservable, which is exactly why it has gone untested. So this
// fixture uses a circuit with THREE.
//
// THE CIRCUIT. One net `p` against ground, carrying a cubic conductance:
//
//     I(p,g) = (V - 1)(V - 2)(V - 3)
//
// Its DC operating points are the roots of that cubic: V = 1, V = 2, V = 3.
// All three are genuine static solutions — a tunnel-diode / latch shape written
// with exact integer roots instead of an exponential, so every number below is
// hand-checkable and no tolerance argument is needed to name the answer.
//
// THE DERIVATION, re-derived for this revision, Newton-Raphson on
// f(V) = V^3 - 6V^2 + 11V - 6 with f'(V) = 3V^2 - 12V + 11 — which is precisely
// the residual and Jacobian the generated device stamps, so this is the
// harness's own iteration. Each line is V, f(V), f'(V), and the step -f/f':
//
//   from V = 0        (no nodeset: the harness seeds x with @splat(0.0))
//     0.000000   -6.000000   11.000000   +0.545455  -> 0.545455
//     0.545455   -1.622840    5.347107   +0.303499  -> 0.848953
//     0.848953   -0.373985    2.974726   +0.125721  -> 0.974674
//     0.974674   -0.052592    2.153880   +0.024417  -> 0.999092
//     0.999092   -0.001819    2.005453   +0.000907  -> 0.999999
//     converges to V = 1
//
//   from V = 2.75     (the nodeset declared below)
//     2.750000   -0.328125    0.687500   +0.477273  -> 3.227273
//     3.227273   +0.621243    3.518595   -0.176560  -> 3.050713
//     3.050713   +0.109271    2.311991   -0.047263  -> 3.003450
//     3.003450   +0.006936    2.020735   -0.003432  -> 3.000018
//     3.000018   +0.000035    2.000106   -0.000018  -> 3.000000
//     converges to V = 3
//
// (The previous revision of this header printed 1.622645, 0.374110/2.975028 and
// "- 0.590.../7.211..." for three of those residuals. Recomputed above; the
// iterates 0.848953, 0.974674, 3.050713 and the conclusion are unchanged.)
//
// So the asserted value is 3.0 and it is reachable ONLY from the nodeset.
// The fixture therefore cannot pass by accident in either direction:
//
//   * ignore the nodeset   -> V = 1,    the assertion fails;
//   * clamp to the nodeset -> V = 2.75, the assertion fails;
//   * honour it as an initial guess -> V = 3, the assertion passes.
//
// That middle line is the whole anti-clamp claim, carried by this one number:
// 3.0 is not 2.75. A previous revision spelled it out a second time as
// `CHECK(V(p,g) - 2.75, 0.25, 1e-9)`; at the same tolerance that is
// algebraically the first assertion rearranged and cannot fail independently of
// it, so it is gone. The anti-clamp property is additionally pinned, on a
// circuit where clamping is the ONLY failure mode, by the already-green
// tests/fixtures/ch03_data_types/21_net_nodeset.va.
//
// 2.75 is 11/4, exact in binary, so the seed carries no rounding of its own.
// The tolerance is 1e-9 and not `CHECKX` only because the harness's ground row
// can settle a denormal off zero; the three roots are a whole unit apart, so
// nothing about which root was reached rides on that tolerance.
//
// ON CONFORMANCE. §3.6.3.2 does not say which of three DC solutions a solver
// shall report; what it says is that the declared number is used AS THE NODESET
// VALUE by the analog solver, i.e. as the potential the DC solve starts from.
// This fixture asserts exactly that and nothing more: it reads the root that
// Newton from 2.75 reaches. An implementation that replaces the declared start
// with its own continuation has not used the initializer as a nodeset value,
// which is the case the clause forbids.
//
// WHY IT FAILS TODAY. codegen emits `pub const u_nodeset = [n_u]?f64{ 2.75,
// null }` for this module (lib/backend/codegen.zig `emitNodesets`), and nothing
// reads it: lib/backend/tb.zig opens every operating point with
// `var x: [n_u]f64 = @splat(0.0);` and the host has no reference to the symbol
// at all. Observed today: `x[p] = 1.000000e0`.
//
//! lrm 3.6.3.2
//! bias V(g) = 0.0
//! solve
//! print none
`include "check.vh"
module a08_nodeset_initial_guess(p, g);
    inout p, g;
    electrical g;
    // §3.6.3.2's own first example shape: `electrical a = 5.0;`.
    electrical p = 2.75;

    analog begin
        `CHECK("the nodeset seeds Newton, so it lands on the high root", V(p, g), 3.0, 1e-9);
        I(p, g) <+ (V(p, g) - 1.0) * (V(p, g) - 2.0) * (V(p, g) - 3.0);
    end
endmodule
